[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039cb004-53ac-537c-ba3f-c9c4fa4d8669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=2{t}^{2},\\,y-3=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351601} {:answer/id #uuid "12f01c71-c3cd-561e-8123-2d2a35da3d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+3=\\frac{1}{8}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351729} {:answer/id #uuid "429f28da-e311-55f4-8ba6-78f24e535f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1=4{t}^{2},\\,y-3=8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352178} {:answer/id #uuid "c12034f2-b90f-5b19-b570-a3a0deecadb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+3=\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353235} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e8a924-fd29-5529-82ad-906db2e56844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+3=\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0e8a924-fd29-5529-82ad-906db2e56844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+3=\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353665} :answer-field/id #uuid "5602969a-9a9b-5dd6-8d1b-6190b295100d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab4e5874-aa56-54b2-bdba-613e8d2b027f" :question/math-academy-id "q-84841" :question/problem "![](images/46/46929b27eb98506479401e2b78ea18fac539a11dcda4ecfb7b90c700dec61fdb.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(1,-3),$ and passes through the point $(2,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,-3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=p{t}^{2},\\,y+3=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(2,-4),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=-4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -1 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ 1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{4p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=(\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+3=2(\\frac{1}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+3=\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62eaa041-2d63-564f-ade1-7bef5fab22d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352447} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8625b2-1c64-5566-ba29-56791d7243e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352633} {:answer/id #uuid "80ea7907-626a-58cd-b76b-d71280582e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352690} {:answer/id #uuid "c47f5bd7-dbb3-5a6d-88bb-4adffd53fa9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353268} {:answer/id #uuid "f96eba66-a4aa-5124-828b-e3a539a0c308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f96eba66-a4aa-5124-828b-e3a539a0c308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353745} :answer-field/id #uuid "097c2732-5843-5ebf-8e23-aff4c65b6b8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6701f02f-0a7c-580d-b91a-d0aa02156717" :question/math-academy-id "q-84842" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7cb71517fe60c9d15b6cacddbf5f91c2e4a60103b8917a4d9baa287517a5fd54.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,-1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,1).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-3,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,1),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=1$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f2b26d-fde2-5df9-a2e8-fdae8ef10556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4={t}^{2},\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352038} {:answer/id #uuid "7cabc6e5-0779-5899-b958-362fdca71d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=-{t}^{2},\\,y+2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352660} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9bf6a7-09a7-5efe-8b9b-430b70534826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,y+2=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352784} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3af50b-9402-558a-b0dd-642bd8ead266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353045} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ce349-4bd8-5347-b4d9-1ea6996af39c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d23ce349-4bd8-5347-b4d9-1ea6996af39c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353387} :answer-field/id #uuid "264530ce-ad30-506a-b28e-7c8f8705a624" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f49ece9d-5819-51fa-b688-22dc0878f927" :question/math-academy-id "q-84843" :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba3d2c8719c97911d2339548f08601b07bcfe555d59dbb058a157836c45a34fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-4,2),$ and passes through the point $(-5,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-4,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+4=(-1){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(-1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e2b79a9-3740-5739-8681-d539714698e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=8(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352674} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe4d2b6-cbc3-5ca9-888b-07738efa5b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=4(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352935} {:answer/id #uuid "bea504b0-64b7-5ec1-9521-a83512f154ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=2(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353198} {:answer/id #uuid "c20b9909-4742-51fc-8f0d-1b3b5f9e5363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=4(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353246} {:answer/id #uuid "daa05f1f-40a6-5233-a72a-88d3e0cb4efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=2(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c20b9909-4742-51fc-8f0d-1b3b5f9e5363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=4(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353246} :answer-field/id #uuid "43ba2752-d196-596b-8465-6532e20b9383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd8e3dfd-6f7e-504a-b98a-c0593b85e82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-84851" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=2t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+3}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4} & =y-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =4(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f9e92b-1b39-59d6-8f27-f72c54b374e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=2(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352181} {:answer/id #uuid "9b252d48-58d6-5fa6-9583-5cd764d89309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=2(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352898} {:answer/id #uuid "c59ddb09-5e49-5a07-8b50-8eed51431933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=8(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353278} {:answer/id #uuid "c9151409-88e5-5199-999e-48743d0cd228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=8(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353317} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d8628c-b1b0-52e4-8ac4-974a9029ec95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=16(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c59ddb09-5e49-5a07-8b50-8eed51431933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=8(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353278} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a9cd5b-884d-552e-a25e-ed74e2373425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3011f06-5149-58bf-a601-8ad1e445dd9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-84853" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=2{t}^{2},\\,y-4=4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+3}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-4}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+3}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x+3}{2} \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =16\\cdot (\\frac{x+3}{2}) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =8(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d065c5-289c-5df3-8e3d-d7536b5b04a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-4(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352165} {:answer/id #uuid "87c495d4-372c-587f-8ba9-248ff4836909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}=4(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352756} {:answer/id #uuid "8aed7c7e-36ae-5b2a-a27f-de5b08c82404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}=8(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352780} {:answer/id #uuid "d245767f-5984-5d49-bf51-6be63d24e319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-2(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353388} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8bd0aa-d5d5-59dd-96ee-bd2a1de7606b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-8(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40d065c5-289c-5df3-8e3d-d7536b5b04a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=-4(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352165} :answer-field/id #uuid "011cb5db-eaf9-5dd0-b601-e87e1e2b2806" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f6ea62c-3d64-50ec-b882-0c7611fa1214" :question/math-academy-id "q-84854" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+5=-2t,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+5}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=-(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(y+2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4} & =-(y+2) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =-4(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1062cd47-9501-5d4d-89f0-1f0dfac46ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-12(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351706} {:answer/id #uuid "203d5265-0735-5f29-a4b3-23f27c01d1ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351855} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4d03b8-7e1a-5f4e-9768-79ee70fb6bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-6(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352144} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f311bc-9e97-5b0a-8b73-8c3ad55e19ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=12(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352975} {:answer/id #uuid "bc15fb90-bb8b-5f3f-b077-f83e5f0a720c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1062cd47-9501-5d4d-89f0-1f0dfac46ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-12(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351706} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c0a390a-4c17-5206-816e-693c33b98e33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39c29706-500e-5209-8aff-2370c6bcdd81" :question/math-academy-id "q-84855" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-3=-6t,\\,y-1=-3{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x-3}{6},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x-3}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-1}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & =-\\frac{y-1}{3} \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =36\\cdot (-\\frac{y-1}{3}) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-12(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30375770-18d1-55c1-a5a0-90428580a7ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=4(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352007} {:answer/id #uuid "5d39139a-8521-5761-b937-c8daafdc3c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-4(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352391} {:answer/id #uuid "96146462-b494-5188-b5e0-00e3b96feefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=8(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352868} {:answer/id #uuid "c281848e-7235-5dbf-ac89-d5be4aba90d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-8(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353250} {:answer/id #uuid "dc28d93c-a83f-5b31-bdd4-7d8d0036bea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-8(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc28d93c-a83f-5b31-bdd4-7d8d0036bea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-8(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353479} :answer-field/id #uuid "d419f509-1b71-5112-aaa0-7be6490a3415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "498096d3-80ed-51eb-b10a-90d7f551479a" :question/math-academy-id "q-84858" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-4}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y-1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y-1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{x-4}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-4}{2} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-4}{2}) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-8(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "288c1c06-2498-55be-af84-64025de5b036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351943} {:answer/id #uuid "4759ba61-c36a-5eeb-bf3c-db6d3982dec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352227} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9c51cd-97fe-5ec1-81c9-933a63d4893c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352260} {:answer/id #uuid "5e61dff6-cf64-5212-9dbd-51badf87ecb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-5(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352402} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5df374-7f86-5a06-97b0-4c7c7a354539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-5(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc5df374-7f86-5a06-97b0-4c7c7a354539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-5(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353169} :answer-field/id #uuid "02cba858-60e5-5d69-a896-3a09d3f84f7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f7f6549-2721-5283-9cbb-bd6bbc1d1262" :question/math-academy-id "q-84859" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5},\\,t=-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-5(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf0e493-1a24-54de-9bab-c202b789b9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351979} {:answer/id #uuid "67032fe8-0613-59f6-b67a-631024afbef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352480} {:answer/id #uuid "6a82c9bf-3523-56e9-84cd-3a60d8e110c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352513} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e8d0c6-5d08-583e-bdb9-7b30862fcbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353282} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ffa7ef-509f-55c6-a7e5-dc4d650b0fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a82c9bf-3523-56e9-84cd-3a60d8e110c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352513} :answer-field/id #uuid "e06ee698-9ed6-56c6-9865-c1cdafef5976" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3834474f-27e5-5548-9ec5-2158ba9e8e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-84953" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=-2y?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(-\\frac{1}{2})y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1345d968-20d1-5ac9-bbfe-3c46ce781861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351732} {:answer/id #uuid "967290dc-71b3-501d-8605-16ee088f7c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352870} {:answer/id #uuid "a4389b55-ae83-53e1-9f8d-a4182b6742a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352968} {:answer/id #uuid "c15bc970-f051-5dfb-a7f1-db97122d6816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353237} {:answer/id #uuid "ded0b80c-41d3-53a3-9730-fea5f6f67367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1345d968-20d1-5ac9-bbfe-3c46ce781861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351732} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7c729ab-9ef7-56a2-b67a-e30b58f4144d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a3d9a2c-1761-5baf-9e5d-4958b26130d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-84954" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=5y?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4(\\frac{5}{4})y.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a7d4fcc-a671-5294-87c0-16f54aa45d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351807} {:answer/id #uuid "303e0b09-de3a-565e-88d0-3832da030fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352008} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8d4caa-5d97-5233-8771-4970735f2db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352397} {:answer/id #uuid "82d5e27e-d1d8-5fe6-90f4-ec4c09575fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352708} {:answer/id #uuid "d75cd6d7-6abb-545c-8ff0-20d78f08988f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d75cd6d7-6abb-545c-8ff0-20d78f08988f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353435} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec3cbf91-65fe-5e03-a9bd-cbbd696e8c91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9c37a31-cbf7-579c-82f4-1ccdb89a382e" :question/math-academy-id "q-84966" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}(y-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{8})(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{3}{8}.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=0$ and $k=1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(0,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454a079a-3b5d-5568-a29d-6f2901c3f216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352210} {:answer/id #uuid "66130b54-d958-5449-b32e-e09731f751e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352466} {:answer/id #uuid "72d2ceff-58e4-5cc6-ae01-b9e7dcc9c275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352571} {:answer/id #uuid "75807951-bdad-56c4-9ac3-85b96c3b3874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answe